Synopsys: A sparse matrix diagonalization scheme was applied to the FDMNES code [Phys. Rev. B, 63, 125120 (2001)]. The required calculation time for XANES spectrum was reduced by more than one order of magnitude compared to the standard Gaussian method and efficient multicore parallelization is now available. Thus structural analysis of complex systems can be performed routinely on a modern personal computer. We analyzed the bending angle between CO and NO molecules attached to Ni active site in the CPO-27-Ni metal-organic framework and the ultrafast low spin (LS) to high spin (HS) conversion of photoexcited aqueous \(\left[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}\right]^{2+}\), which - next to the initial and final states - also includes the XANES calculation for the recently identified transient triplet state.
